// ADR 0025 S1b — THE ABUSE DIRECTION OF THE SPELLABILITY LAYER.
//
// ⚠ THIS FILE REPLACES `fail/ctr_leaf_family_vector_refused`, AND WHY IT HAD TO
// MOVE RATHER THAN BE WEAKENED OR DELETED. That fixture pinned the same abuse
// direction on a `kind vector` family, whose emitter arm produced no leaf
// batches. S1b GAVE the vector arm a leaf-batch producer, so its substantive
// claim ("a vector family cannot be leaf-walked") became FALSE — the subject was
// implemented, which is the one thing that retires a refusal. The abuse
// direction itself did not retire, so it is re-aimed here at a family that still
// genuinely produces no batches. The admitting side of the vector arm is
// `pass/ctr_vec_leaf_family_spelling`.
//
// `CtrLeafFamily` is a SEPARATE trait from `CtrFamily` for one measured reason:
// `impl CtrFamily` is emitted for ordered_map, multimap AND vector families,
// while `{N}LeafBatch`/`{N}LeafWalk` come out of the FSE ordered_map arm and the
// vector arm ONLY. The VOLUME arm — an ordered_map whose value is `str`, i.e.
// VLE storage — emits neither, because a volume family's value is a `&[u8]` and
// projects as a `str` rel column: a second batch shape, not a harder one, and
// not this slice's. Putting the two assoc types on `CtrFamily` was MEASURED and
// it breaks such a family's OWN emission — `error [impl CtrFamily for
// CtrClass$G1$hs_…]: unknown type 'Hs…LeafBatch'`.
//
// So the capability is a membership (ADR §3: existence = trait membership), and
// THIS is the direction that has to be checked: a family that produces no leaf
// batches must not end up with a leaf walk.
//
// ⚠ WHERE THE REFUSAL ACTUALLY LANDS, MEASURED, AND THE HOLE THAT LEAVES.
// The refusal below is at the METHOD (`'Hs…' has no method 'leaf_batches'`),
// NOT at the projection. The projection `<typeof(Vol1) as CtrLeafFamily>::LeafWalk`
// is NOT refused for an unsatisfied obligation — it resolves to `<error>` and the
// compile continues (`mlir_gen: unresolved AssocType '<error>::CtrLeafFamily::
// LeafWalk' — mono_pass required`). So the type-level obligation on an assoc-type
// projection is UNCHECKED today; this test pins the refusal that exists, and the
// missing one is recorded here rather than left as an assumption. What the test
// does hold is the substantive claim — a volume family cannot be leaf-walked —
// which is the ground for splitting the trait in the first place.
//
// THE OTHER DIRECTION OF THAT SPLIT IS ALREADY IN THE CORPUS TWICE:
// `pass/ctr_leaf_family_spelling` (FSE ordered_map) and
// `pass/ctr_vec_leaf_family_spelling` (vector) both project the same two assoc
// types and answer. Neither half alone separates "refuses the right thing" from
// "refuses everything". And `pass/metaclass_str_generic` builds a volume family
// through the factory and must keep compiling, so the arrangement VERDICT 4
// measured as broken — the two assoc types on `CtrFamily` itself — reds a LIVE
// test, not only a probe.
package ctr_leaf_family_volume_refused;

use logos.lcm.canon.container_item;
use logos.lcm.canon.metaclass;
use logos.mem.bt.map;
use logos.mem.bt.memstore;
use logos.mem.pkd;
use logos.mem.sync;
use logos.lang.str;
use logos.lang.writ.wstatic;

pub container Vol1 {
    kind ordered_map;
    entry { key: u64, val: str }
    measure max(key);
}

// A volume family has no leaf-batch producer, so it is not a `CtrLeafFamily`.
pub type VolWalk = <typeof(Vol1) as CtrLeafFamily>::LeafWalk;

fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut store: Arc<MemoryStore> = store_new();
    let snap: Arc<dyn Snapshot> = store_fork_arc(&mut store);
    let mut c = create_ctr::<typeof(Vol1)>(&snap, 1u64);
    let mut s: VolWalk = c.leaf_batches();
    return 0i32;
}
